#{[17592186581948 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=2"] [17592186581961 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {(-4)}^{+}}f(x)=16"] [17592186581976 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=2"] [17592186581991 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {(-4)}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [17592186582016 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [17592186582020 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)={e}^{2}"] [17592186582046 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {(-4)}^{+}}f(x)=-4"] [17592186582120 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=2"] [17592186582145 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=1"] [17592186582199 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {(-4)}^{+}}f(x)=4"] [17592186582218 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=0"] [17592186582222 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=e"] [17592186582345 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186582350 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-3).$"] [17592186582350 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-3).$\n\n![](images/14/145145a2e750f9eec3d6a010846fcb46fa700878d3cae28e8db315e451273dd2.png)\n\nAs $x$ decreases, the graph oscillates between the values $y=-1$ and $y=1$ indefinitely. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-3)=DNE.\n$$"] [17592186582364 :question/problem "Find the limit at $x=1$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-1\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<1 \\\\ 2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x=1 \\\\ 2-{x}^{2}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186582365 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi }\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186582368 :question/problem "Find the right-sided limit at $x=32$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 32 \\\\ \\sqrt[5]{x}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {32}^{+}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186582371 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /6)^{+}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186582382 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$"] [17592186582410 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /{3}^{+}}({cos}^{2}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186582415 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)^{+}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x\\pi )=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186582416 :question/problem "Find the left-sided and the right-sided limits at $x=4$ for the function\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2 & \\text{ for }\\,x<4 \\\\ {e}^{x-4}+1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186583002 :question/problem "$\\lim_{t\\to {0}^{+}}v(t)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}"] [17592186583003 :question/problem "Consider a vector-valued function $f(u)$ that is continuous over all real numbers and the real-valued function $u(t)=\\sqrt{3t+7}.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to -1}f(u(t))=\n$$"] [17592186583014 :question/problem "Consider a vector-valued function $f(u)$ that is continuous over all real numbers and the real-valued function $u(t)={t}^{2}+1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(u(t))=\n$$"] [17592186583025 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$ and $f(2)=-3,$ what is the value of\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}{({f}^{2}(x)-3x)}^{3}\\,?\n$$"] [17592186583064 :question/problem "$\\lim_{t\\to -1}\\frac{{t}^{2}-8t-9}{{t}^{2}-5t-6}=$"] [17592186583078 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{t\\to \\pi /12}{v}_{2}(t)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}"] [17592186583583 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi }\\frac{5f(x)+1}{(2x+\\pi )^{2}}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi }\\frac{5f(x)+1}{(2x+\\pi )^{2}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186583590 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{\\pi }^{+}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{\\pi }^{+}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186583596 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-7}\\frac{(x+7)f(x)}{{x}^{2}+5x-14}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\,-7}\\frac{(x+7)f(x)}{{x}^{2}+5x-14}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)$"] [17592186583597 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=-8$ and $f(-8)=25,$ then what is \n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-8)^{-}}\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{f(x)}}?\n$$"] [17592186583604 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-7}\\frac{x+7}{{x}^{2}+5x-14}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\,-7}\\frac{x+7}{{x}^{2}+5x-14}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186583623 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the right-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nThe function has three discontinuities: $x=-2$, $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\n- At $x=-2$ the function is neither left- nor right-continuous because it's undefined at this point.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function is left-continuous but is not right-continuous because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=0\\ne -1=f(0).\n$$\n\n- At $x=1$ the function is right-continuous because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=2=f(1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=1$ only."] [17592186583631 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186583635 :question/problem "![](images/dc/dce065da5fb6e0ebb09537b27e108801d61c80c4905dcc8ae54f169dfdbd4f0f.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(h)$ is shown above, where\n\n$$\ng(h)=\\frac{4}{h}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{h}{2}\\right)-1).\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, what is $\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)?$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186583635 :question/worked-solution "The graph shows that as we approach $h=0$ from the left, the $y$ value approaches $0.$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(h)=0.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $h=0$ from the right, the $y$ value approaches $0.$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(h)=0.\n$$\n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $0,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=0.\n$$"] [17592186583642 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186583642 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right).$\n\n![](images/44/446f0fd704e09106f34a70fcbd3adb2806bfea13664114bd5d704fd3c8519001.png)\n\nAs $x$ decreases to $-\\infty$, the graph oscillates between the values $y=-4$ and $y=4$ indefinitely. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=DNE.\n$$"] [17592186585049 :question/problem "The financial director predicts that, in the future, the profit will have an overall behavior similar to the function $\\sqrt[3]{4x-4}.$ Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{4x-4}.$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{4x-4}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186585049 :question/worked-solution "Let $k(x)=\\sqrt[3]{4x-4}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=k(x):$\n\n![](images/c5/c5216901041a8042a8ec3b6d444c37ab95488414715dbca923c008ef3ccc0bf0.png)\n\nThe graph shows that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }k(x)=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186587299 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to 1}||f(s)||$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{s}^{2}}{s-1},\\frac{6s-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(s-1)}{3s-2},\\frac{{s}^{3}+4s-2}{{s}^{2}+s-1}⟩.\n$$"] [17592186587308 :question/problem "The economist predicts that, in the future, the inflation will have an overall behavior similar to the function $\\sqrt[3]{x-4}.$ Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{x-4}.$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{x-4}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186587308 :question/worked-solution "Let $k(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x-4}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=k(x):$\n\n![](images/22/22d381f28b74d851369a40ef1c1f5bb6ef7fb22c11a7afb0145587480c0a7ccb.png)\n\nThe graph shows that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }k(x)=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186588205 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\sqrt[3]{x}=\n$$"] [17592186588205 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/35/35cd7d1487285bcb2094325ab052a2a2eb13dfe8191ec125118387bc9d7a93d5.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186588222 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}-2x}.$"] [17592186591640 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)$ is undefined\n\nII. $\\lim_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*"] [17592186591667 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$ for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+1),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty$\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0$"] [17592186591718 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0$ at $\\theta =0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0$\n\n*Hint: Use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*"] [17592186592281 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 16}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-4}{x-16}.$"] [17592186593386 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0"] [17592186593431 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [17592186593507 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [17592186593526 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,-3}f(x)=-1"] [17592186593696 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=1"] [17592186593699 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,-3}f(x)=-2"] [17592186593715 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=0"] [17592186593719 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,-3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [17592186593832 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,-3}f(x)=2"] [17592186593855 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=-2"] [17592186593873 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=2"] [17592186593890 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=-1"] [17592186593915 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=1"] [17592186593939 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,-3}f(x)=1"] [17592186593954 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=-2"] [17592186594152 :question/problem "![](images/c5/c5e03bbe06b44f27a9573e9dbfb8efbf0f1f1ee8efe6e8093dfbe64bb431d790.png)\n\nThe function  $y=f(x)$ is defined on $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x).$"] [17592186594152 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that it is not possible to approach $x=0$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nWhen we approach $x=0$ from the left, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x),$ we  conclude  that\n \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186594171 :question/problem "![](images/f9/f98551299f73f55e9c1a07ef513723f068f66576a514d1895c07f317c10ae262.png)\n\nThe function  $y=f(x)$ is defined on $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup (0,\\infty ).$ Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x).$"] [17592186594171 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that it is not possible to approach $x=-2$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nWhen we approach $x=-2$ from the left, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nSince $\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x),$ we  conclude  that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186594187 :question/problem "![](images/88/884ec8c4d7e079915b7b6a21a2008514bc201580b447dcfc0f9c4007e60d00dc.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=-2$\n\nII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nIII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=-1$"] [17592186594187 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=-2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Both limits $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$ do not exist because the function $f(x)$ is not given in the interval $[0,2).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $2$ from the left and right we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x).$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."] [17592186594197 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{3{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}-2}.$"] [17592186594197 :question/worked-solution "For any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{P(x)}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $3{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}-2$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{3{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}-2}=0.\n$$"] [17592186594219 :question/problem "![](images/d6/d651c43faf3d85411e3aefa905278ff64e45b178f5625a0b92c7c89c3b5eb66f.png)\n\nThe function  $y=f(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-3}f(x).$"] [17592186594219 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that it is not possible to approach $x=-3$ from the left. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, when we approach $x=-3$ from the right, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nSince $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{+}}f(x),$ we  conclude  that\n \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186594395 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=2$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$"] [17592186594411 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [17592186594437 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,\\lim_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] [17592186594471 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=0"] [17592186594543 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [17592186594670 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)=\n$$"] [17592186594670 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3).$\n\n![](images/65/65420a63daa7a7c4f7a4c061bfc4e7fd223d28cc90f7fbc7e02d4d6f066ec968.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186594671 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}{log}_{8}⁡(4-x)=\n$$"] [17592186594671 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{8}⁡(4-x).$\n\n![](images/0b/0b3cd9b5d4110b8b76b3e165d75ee4d8019cc9cc17192f38e9f2751350e5ddc7.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\ge 4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}{log}_{8}⁡(4-x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x<4.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}{log}_{8}⁡(4-x)=-\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186594673 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{({x}^{2}-3)^{2}-8x+15}{{x}^{2}-4x+4}=$"] [17592186594677 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,\\lim_{x\\to 16}\\frac{{x}^{2}-20x+64}{\\sqrt{x}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186594701 :question/problem "Find the right-sided limit at $x=2$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-16\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2x+5}-\\sqrt{2x}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186594707 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to 4}{log}_{5}⁡(4-x).$"] [17592186594707 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{5}⁡(4-x).$\n\n![](images/4c/4c8119ac59d4fe8102a4d78e10f32f1b3f0116366001b6a78d256b3928867943.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\ge 4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}{log}_{5}⁡(4-x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x<4.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}{log}_{5}⁡(4-x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 4}{log}_{5}⁡(4-x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186594715 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{8x}-{e}^{5x}-3x}{{x}^{2}}=$"] [17592186594719 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{ln\\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186595927 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to (-5/2)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+5).$"] [17592186595927 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+5)$\n\n![](images/f4/f43a774fdfe638719972b74163adc61dc1099ab0117bda1c5bcc648912fd7b25.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le -\\frac{5}{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -5/{2}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+5)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>-\\frac{5}{2}.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -5/{2}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+5)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -5/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+5)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186595955 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}{(x+1)^{2}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186595955 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $(x+1)^{2}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}{(x+1)^{2}}=0.\n$$"] [17592186595957 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{log}_{5}⁡(x-2).$"] [17592186595957 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{5}⁡(x-2).$\n\n![](images/42/42585a4ff6db9c4848588f10ac50bc78aa4d902ddacdb201039aa7a558d3a3a9.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{log}_{5}⁡(x-2)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>2.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{log}_{5}⁡(x-2)=-\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186595959 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{6x}{{e}^{x}}+3).$"]}
